\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Model and data.}
We use DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B~\citep{deepseek2025r1}, a 7B-parameter reasoning model with Grouped Query Attention (28 query heads, 4 KV groups per layer, $G{=}7$) and FFN intermediate dimension 18944.
All experiments use GSM8K~\citep{cobbe2021gsm8k}: 512 training examples for calibration, 1319 test problems for evaluation with greedy decoding and exact-match accuracy.

\paragraph{Pruning methods.}
We compare three conditions at 20\% structured sparsity:
(1)~\textbf{Unpruned}: the original model (upper bound);
(2)~\textbf{Standard}: importance-only pruning via Taylor expansion $I(h) = |w_h \cdot g_h|$, ranking and removing the lowest-scoring structures globally;
(3)~\textbf{RASP}: recovery-aware scoring $S(h) = I(h) \times (1-F(h))^\alpha$ with $\alpha{=}1.0$, where $F(h)$ is the intra-layer CKA functional overlap.
At 20\% sparsity, all 200 pruned structures are MLP channel groups (0/112 attention GQA groups are pruned under any method), as attention groups have uniformly higher importance.
A per-layer capacity floor ensures ${\geq}40\%$ MLP retention per layer.

\paragraph{Recovery pipeline.}
All pruned models undergo identical two-stage recovery:
\emph{SFT warmup} (2 epochs, lr=$2{\times}10^{-5}$, effective batch size 8, GSM8K training data) to restore EOS generation and format compliance, followed by \emph{GRPO alignment}~\citep{shao2024grpo} (8-bit AdamW, lr=$1{\times}10^{-6}$, $\beta_\text{KL}{=}0.01$, temperature 0.4, repetition penalty 1.15, format reward $+0.1$ for \texttt{\textbackslash boxed\{\}}, max completion length 1024, 100 steps) on 2$\times$NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 (96GB).
The unpruned model undergoes the same SFT$\to$GRPO pipeline for controlled comparison.

\subsection{GRPO Reward-Accuracy Decoupling}
\label{sec:decoupling}

Table~\ref{tab:main} presents our central finding: \textbf{GRPO reward improvement does not translate to accuracy gain for any model post-SFT.}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{GRPO reward-accuracy decoupling. All accuracy values: $N{=}1319$, GSM8K, greedy, seed=42. GRPO Acc is measured at the best available checkpoint before or at collapse onset.}
\label{tab:main}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccl@{}}
\toprule
Model & SFT Acc & GRPO Acc & $\Delta$ & Peak Reward & Collapse \\
\midrule
Unpruned & 81.27 & 81.27 & 0.00 & 0.661 & None \\
Standard 20\% & 59.29 & 58.91 & $-$0.38 & 0.096 & Transient \\
RASP 20\% & 61.49 & 60.65 & $-$0.84 & 0.039 & Permanent \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The unpruned model provides the strongest evidence: over 100 GRPO steps, reward rises from 0 to 0.661---a 100-fold increase---yet accuracy remains at exactly 81.27\%, with the \emph{identical} 1072 out of 1319 test problems answered correctly before and after GRPO.
This is not a failure to detect small improvements: the pre- and post-GRPO models produce the same set of correct answers.
GRPO's reward signal, which measures whether sampled completions reach the correct answer during training, does not encode improvements in greedy-decoded reasoning chains.

For pruned models, the pattern holds with an additional pathology: accuracy slightly \emph{decreases} under GRPO ($-0.38$pp for Standard, $-0.84$pp for RASP).
The reward signal is correspondingly weaker (peak 0.096 and 0.039 vs.\ 0.661 for unpruned), but the critical observation is that even the modest reward gains fail to translate to accuracy improvement.
This reward-accuracy decoupling appears universal under our experimental conditions: GRPO training on GSM8K with greedy evaluation, regardless of whether the model is pruned or intact.

\subsection{Collapse Dynamics and Severity Gradient}
\label{sec:collapse}

While reward-accuracy decoupling is universal, pruning introduces a qualitatively distinct pathology: \textbf{GRPO training collapse}---the complete failure of the RL training process, characterized by vanishing gradients, degenerate sampling, and cessation of meaningful policy updates.
The three models exhibit a clean severity gradient that diagnoses structural damage type.

\paragraph{Collapse metrics.}
We track four diagnostic signals at each training step: (1)~mean reward across rollouts, (2)~\texttt{frac\_zero\_std}---the fraction of prompts where all rollout rewards are identical (indicating no exploration signal), (3)~gradient norm, and (4)~mean terminated completion length.
Table~\ref{tab:collapse} summarizes the collapse trajectory for each model.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{GRPO training collapse metrics by pruning method. Collapse onset = first step where $\texttt{frac\_zero\_std} \geq 0.85$ or $\texttt{grad\_norm} < 0.05$.}
\label{tab:collapse}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}
\toprule
Model & Onset & Peak Reward & Final $f_\text{zero}$ & Final $\|\nabla\|$ & Recovery \\
\midrule
Unpruned & --- & 0.669 & $\leq$0.275 & 6.97 & N/A \\
Standard & Step 60 & 0.096 & 0.475 & 2.14 & Yes \\
RASP & Step 10 & 0.039 & 0.950 & 0.007 & No \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Unpruned: stable training.}
The unpruned model trains stably for all 100 steps.
Reward rises monotonically from 0 to 0.661 (peak 0.669 at step 80), gradient norms remain healthy (2.45--14.94), KL divergence is tightly bounded (0.0046--0.0053), and \texttt{frac\_zero\_std} never exceeds 0.275.
A shallow reward dip at step 30 (0.425) recovers by step 40.
This confirms that GRPO itself is functional on intact architectures---collapse is pruning-specific.

\paragraph{Standard pruning: transient instability.}
Standard-pruned models follow a three-phase trajectory: (1)~initial learning (steps 1--40, reward peaks at 0.096, \texttt{frac\_zero\_std}$\approx$0.45, healthy gradients at 3.45); (2)~transient collapse at step 60 (gradient norm drops to 0.013, \texttt{frac\_zero\_std} rises to 0.525); (3)~recovery by step 90 (gradient norm returns to 2.14, reward stabilizes at 0.065).
The step-50 checkpoint evaluation ($N{=}1319$) yields 58.91\%---0.38pp below SFT---indicating that even the pre-collapse training phase failed to improve reasoning accuracy.

\paragraph{RASP pruning: permanent collapse.}
RASP-pruned models collapse catastrophically within 10 steps and never recover.
By step 10, \texttt{frac\_zero\_std} reaches 0.875 and reward is 0.015; by step 30, \texttt{frac\_zero\_std} rises to 0.95, clipped ratio hits 0.956 (nearly all completions truncated at max length), and the effective gradient signal has vanished.
The step-5 evaluation ($N{=}1319$) shows 60.65\%---0.84pp below SFT---confirming that even the brief 5-step pre-collapse window does not improve accuracy.
[TBD: Step-10 evaluation may show a brief pre-collapse improvement window; intermediate results suggest accuracy temporarily rises before permanent collapse destroys it.]

\paragraph{Severity gradient interpretation.}
The 6$\times$ acceleration from Standard (step 60) to RASP (step 10) is directly attributable to their pruning criteria.
RASP preferentially removes high-overlap (functionally redundant) structures---those whose computation is shared with within-layer peers.
We hypothesize that these redundant structures serve as \emph{exploration buffers} during RL training: backup computational paths that allow the policy to explore without catastrophic parameter updates.
Standard pruning, which is overlap-blind, retains some redundant structures by chance, preserving partial exploration capacity and delaying collapse.
The collapse severity gradient thus serves as a zero-cost diagnostic: faster collapse indicates more aggressive removal of structures critical for RL stability.

\subsection{Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff}
\label{sec:tradeoff}

The preceding sections reveal that CKA-aware pruning has \emph{opposite effects} depending on the recovery paradigm:

\paragraph{SFT recovery: RASP advantage.}
Under SFT, RASP-pruned models recover to 61.49\% versus 59.29\% for Standard---a +2.2pp advantage ($N{=}1319$, $p{=}0.25$ by two-proportion $z$-test, not statistically significant at this sample size).
The direction is consistent with the original RASP design rationale: removing redundant structures whose function can be redistributed through gradient-based learning, while protecting unique structures that SFT cannot recreate.

\paragraph{RLVR stability: RASP harmful.}
Under GRPO, the same CKA-aware pruning that marginally benefits SFT causes 6$\times$ faster collapse (step 10 vs.\ step 60).
Functional redundancy, which is expendable for supervised redistribution, provides critical exploration stability for RL---backup paths that prevent policy collapse during reward-driven optimization.

\paragraph{The tradeoff.}
This creates a fundamental \textbf{Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff}: the structures optimal to prune for SFT recovery (high-overlap, functionally redundant) are exactly those needed for RLVR training stability.
No single pruning strategy can simultaneously optimize for both recovery paradigms.
This is consistent with the ``RL Squeezes, SFT Expands'' finding~\citep{rlsqueezes2026}---RLVR recovers through global exploration (requiring structural diversity for stable policy search), while SFT recovers through local trajectory imitation (requiring only that the target function is representable).

\subsection{Ablation: CKA Overlap Weight}
\label{sec:ablation}

We vary the overlap penalty weight $\alpha \in \{0, 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0\}$ in the RASP scoring formula $S(h) = I(h) \times (1-F(h))^\alpha$, where $\alpha{=}0$ reduces to importance-only (Standard-equivalent) pruning.
Table~\ref{tab:ablation} reports post-prune accuracy ($N{=}100$, 512 calibration examples).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Post-prune GSM8K accuracy by CKA weight $\alpha$ ($N{=}100$, 20\% sparsity).}
\label{tab:ablation}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}cccS[table-format=4.0]@{}}
\toprule
$\alpha$ & Acc (\%) & EOS Rate & {Avg Len} \\
\midrule
0.0 & 27 & 0.80 & 1192 \\
0.25 & 23 & 0.63 & 1834 \\
0.5 & 22 & 0.81 & 1141 \\
1.0 & 31 & 0.92 & 733 \\
2.0 & 36 & 0.59 & 2130 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The relationship between $\alpha$ and post-prune accuracy is non-monotonic (U-shaped): moderate overlap penalty ($\alpha{=}0.25$--$0.5$) performs worse than either extreme.
At $\alpha{\geq}1.0$, stronger CKA weighting improves immediate post-prune reasoning (31--36\% vs.\ 22--27\% for low $\alpha$), confirming that overlap-aware group selection preserves reasoning-critical structures.
However, this improved post-prune accuracy comes at the cost of faster GRPO collapse---higher $\alpha$ removes more redundant structures, reducing the exploration buffer available for RL training.
The U-shape suggests a phase transition in group selection: below a threshold overlap weight, the CKA signal introduces noise relative to importance-only ranking; above it, overlap-based reranking consistently selects more reasoning-preserving (but RL-destabilizing) pruning configurations.

